ovie the substitution here is Jake's  family but I don't think it's as strong  as the wonder like awe the first avatar  was able to capture that's kind of here  with the beautiful visuals but not  narratively the characters that we  follow have very little character  development and the main emphasis is on  the spectacle to me this creates a  feeling of disconnection because instead  of having a symbiotic relationship  between dazzling visuals and good  character work we instead have minimal  character work with a stronger emphasis  on visuals during certain segments of  the movie it felt like I was watching an  hour-long version of the ride flight of  passage in Animal Kingdom it was more  like an experience rather than an  engrossing story and again that  experience was very fun but you don't  have to sacrifice the story and just  have scenes solely there for the sake of  spectacle instead you could do both like  in the first avatar another thing is  that Jake Sully and atiri take it  backseat and it's Siri More Than Jake in  that regard so that their kids can take  center stage now this would be fine in  theory if the kids were as interesting  as Jake and natiri were in the first  movie to me this isn't the case firstly  going into the bad dialogue Jake's kids  are talking as if there are some weird  human kids saying bro and cuz in every  other sentence this was very jarring to  say the least and it doesn't give off  the notion that these are Navi now I  know that they have a human father  figure but I find it weird for Jake to  teach his Navi children human slang  words that not even kids use that often  it feels like James Cameron is out of  touch with how kids sound let alone  alien kids you'd think that natiri would  want them to be more skewed toward Navi  culture and would probably dislike that  then moving away from the dialogue the  kids in most scenes didn't come across  as that likable the two brothers had an  extremely basic older younger brother  Dynamic and were constantly coming  across as arrogant that's fine at times  but when they're putting themselves in  Mortal danger to the point where they  need to be saved in every other scene  then it becomes annoying narratively  like the kids were taking prisoner on  three separate occasions once in the  First Act and then twice on the boat  there was a point in the end where they  were like we have to intervene and they  take their youngest sibling with them  for literally no reason I was extremely  confused by that decision because she  offers no value and they are necessarily  putting more of their family in danger  the two child characters that I thought  were more interest thing where spider  and Curie for Kiri it was kind of hard  for me to get over the fact that she was  being played by a 73 year old Sigourney  Weaver but she never came across as  overly annoying the mechanic in which  they can control certain parts of the  world of Pandora was the cold direction  to take her then the inherent nature of  spiders Dynamic of being a human raised  by the navi was fascinating then to have  him be captured by his resurrected  Avatar father korich throws on another  complex Lair that in turn makes for some  good scenes the only issue here plot  wise for this character is at the end of  the movie throughout the course of  spider's Journey he sees how ruthless  and cruel quartz is to the navi there  are some brief moments where he kind of  bonds with his father and opens up a bit  with him but that gets drastically  overshadowed later on by the horrifying  things quartz does also it feels pretty  underdeveloped how an interior was so  quick to kill a spider during the  hostage negotiation in the beginning the  writers kind of drop it and don't  address it again seems like this would  be something that would heavily affect  spiders loyalty so the ending in which  spider saves quartz's life seems pretty  confusing overall and it feels like he's  dooming the fate of th