py their minds contradicts  the need for the orange glob of the  Aging this is a big problem to me  because this is the main objective of  the antagonist then going into some  other weird plot points is that the  humans now want to colonize Pandora this  sounds pretty ridiculous because firstly  why would humans pick a planet with no  oxygen and that also has a hostile  population that they will have to fully  exterminate to live in wouldn't it be  easier to pick a different planet at  this point like it made sense in the  first movie to go to Pandora because it  was rich and unobtanium and they were  just there to mine it but to choose to  live there seems like an odd Choice it  just feels like James Cameron is forcing  the humans to come back on very slim and  unrealistic motivations just so that we  could have this conflict so reworking  their motivations and reasoning for  being there seems like a given and it  can be as simple as there's no more on  obtainium on Earth and they need it on  Pandora and that's why they came back  not to live there permanently and  instead to essentially wage war on  Pandora for all its resources I know  that James Cameron is making a statement  about how the Earth is dying and we need  to find a new planet but when you have  the means of interstellar travel then  surely we'd have more options than just  panda Dora then to list off a couple of  other weird plot points are to see  people disappearing halfway through the  third act when the eclipse happens and  then Jake saying that he needs to  protect the forest Navi Clan by leaving  and then he just joins a new clan that  he then endangers with his presence  seems like a forced way to get to the  Sea I'm also kind of surprised that the  humans didn't care at all about the  forest Clan and only pursued Jake then  one last thing in regard to the script  is that the movie really didn't need to  be over three hours a lot of the scenes  felt pretty extended and could have been  shortened a little although it is really  nice to have some breathtaking visuals  to gawk over it's just that as I said  before you need to be more productive  and have plot progression that coincides  with the spectacle the structure of the  movie also feels a little lopsided  because the First Act was pretty fast  paced in comparison to the long second  act it's pretty apparent that James  Cameron wanted to get to the Sea as soon  as possible then there's one scene that  could probably be deleted in which  quartz's team wants to tame Mountain  banshees the reason why I say this is  because because they don't really  utilize them after this because they  just joined the big ship and park them  for the rest of the movie choir says  briefly use it during the final act  against Jake but you don't need that  small moment in an already big action  set piece this is why I think the  producers were scared to stand up to  James Cameron because there's some fluff  here that could have been cut out  considering that the movie is 3 hours  and 12 minutes I know I've been talking  about all the negatives within the story  but there are still some shining  positives my favorite parts are  definitely the strong emotional moments  between Jake and interior that really  carried the movie at times Zoe Saldana  in particular really blew me away with  her performance and I'm pretty sad that  she wasn't in the movie all that much  then the whale poaching was so  heartbreaking the movie really gets you  to love the tolkan by making them  friendly and sentient creatures making  the aspect of humans hunting them even  more depressing definitely a great  element in the story that was kind of  tarnished by the orange goo aspect  overall the story for Avatar the way of  water feels like a step down in  comparison to the first avatar which I  was kind of surprised by it doesn't feel  as coherent and solid as the first movie  and I feel like the script needed a  little reworking despite being in  development for so long but the action  was stunning and the VFX will easily win  an Oscar with no competition despite the  lackluster story I'd still give it a 3.5  out of 5 on letterboxed thank you for  watching and let me know what you  thought about Avatar the way of water

